EXT. KEYSTONE FLATS TELEGRAPH OFFICE - DAWN

White alkali dust runs to the horizon in every direction. The telegraph office is a single plank room squatting on stilts above the salt, its shadow long and blue. A wire hums overhead like something thinking.

SILAS CROWDER, sixty, sheriff, with a mustache gone the color of ash, leans on the porch rail with a tin cup. MAUDE TALLCHIEF, thirties, the telegraph operator, stands at the window in a man's duster, one hand on the sounder key.

                    SILAS
          Maude, how long since the line went quiet?

                    MAUDE
          Since midnight, Silas. Whoever cut it did it clean.

Beneath the porch, EARLY BRANNIGAN, nineteen, deputy, and still wearing his Sunday hat, sits cross-legged on the ground sharpening a pencil with a pocketknife. He glances up at the sheriff with the hopeful eyes of a dog.

                    EARLY
          Want me to ride out and find the break, Sheriff?

                    SILAS
          Not alone, Early.

A voice rolls in from beyond the last fence post, rich and unhurried, a preacher's voice carried on a cold wind.

                    ABEL (O.S.)
          Sheriff Crowder! I've come about the wire.

Silas sets down his cup. Maude draws a revolver from the folds of her coat. Early stands so quickly his pencil snaps.

                    SILAS
          Abel Whitcomb. Last I heard you were burying folks in Dry Fork.

                    ABEL (O.S.)
          Buried a few. Marry a few. This morning I'm doing neither.

A silhouette appears at the edge of the flat: a man in black walking with great patience, a coil of copper wire over one shoulder.

                    MAUDE
          That's my wire.

                    ABEL (O.S.)
          It was lying in the road, Miss Tallchief. I figured someone was missing it.

Early lets out a laugh, brittle and unsure, and Maude glares at him. Silas steps down off the porch into the dust.

                    SILAS
          Early. Put your hat straight.

                    EARLY
          Yes, Sheriff.

                    SILAS
          Now go inside and ask Maude to send a message.

                    MAUDE
          Silas, the line is dead.

                    SILAS
          Then we'll send it the old way.

He raises his hand to the sky. The wire above them sings once, a high clean note, and falls silent.
